## AI plugin

Done 2026-10-19: libtorch is loaded only on the first AI step, from a plugin library next to the program (`ai/cpu`, `ai/cuda`). The app no longer links libtorch.

- One crate, `crates/ai_engine` (`evanalyzer_ai_engine`), one module per part:
  - `api` - engine-neutral: `Request`/`Task` (one variant per model kind), `Response`, `EngineConfig`, the `InferenceEngine` trait, the C interface (`eva_ai_api_version`, `eva_ai_init`, `eva_ai_infer`, `eva_ai_free`; postcard bytes, image by pointer).
  - `torch` (feature `torch`, the only code with `tch`): scheduler (device slots, per-device model cache, OOM retry), the four models' tensor code, the C exports (`catch_unwind`, log forwarding).
  - `runtime` - plugin loader (feature `torch-plugin`) and CUDA driver probe; the crate root picks the engine once: `torch` linked in (tests) > the plugin > "no AI model support". Plugin search: `EVANALYZER_AI_PLUGIN`, next to the program, `ai/cuda` (driver sees a GPU, CPU not asked for), `ai/cpu`, then `ai/cuda` (CUDA libtorch on the CPU). CUDA probe through the NVIDIA driver (`cuInit` + `cuDeviceGetCount`), not libtorch.
  - The plugin = this crate as a cdylib with `torch`: `cargo build-ai-plugin` (`cargo rustc --crate-type cdylib`; no cdylib in the manifest, or every build depending on the crate would link a torch-less copy).
- Features: core/app/bin `libtorch` (bin default); the old `ai` feature is gone - trained classifiers (Burn, smartcore) are always built.
- Another engine (e.g. Burn once the models are converted) = a module with an `InferenceEngine` and one arm in `start` (crates/ai_engine/src/lib.rs); the model steps only build `Request`s.

Open:
- [ ] Smoke test the packages on real machines: "no plugin", "CPU plugin", "CUDA plugin without GPU", "CUDA plugin with GPU" - Linux, Windows (DLL search flags), macOS (install names, ad-hoc signing of `ai/cpu`).
- [x] **No more `torch_cuda` relink** (2026-10-19): the app loads `libtorch_cuda.so` / `torch_cuda.dll` from the plugin's folder right after the plugin, before its first libtorch call (it registers CUDA when loaded; the CPU build has none) - `crates/ai_engine/build.rs` is gone, the release checks now check that the CUDA library is packaged in `ai/cuda`. Test: `the_cuda_library_next_to_the_plugin_is_loaded_with_it`. Still to confirm on a GPU machine (smoke test above): CUDA available, engine "libtorch (CUDA)". Note: with libtorch linked into a test binary (`torch` feature) CUDA is no longer registered there - those tests run on the CPU.
- [ ] The Windows plugin build (`cargo xwin rustc … --crate-type cdylib`) has not run yet - first release CI run.
- [ ] **Windows CUDA: "Cannot load symbol cudnnGetVersion"** (user report 2026-10-19, Cellpose-SAM) - fixed, not yet confirmed: libtorch loads cuDNN / nvrtc only at the first inference, with the process' DLL search, which didn't include `ai\cuda`. The plugin folder is now added for every later load (`SetDllDirectoryW` + `AddDllDirectory`, `search_later_loads_in` in crates/ai_engine/src/runtime/plugin.rs). Linux is safe by construction (libtorch_cuda NEEDs libcudnn; every cudnn lib has RUNPATH $ORIGIN); macOS has no CUDA.
- [x] **No second libtorch** (2026-10-19): only a plugin that fails to *load* lets the next one be tried; one that loads but fails to set up is set up again for the CPU (CUDA libtorch runs there too), else it is the error - its library is kept (leaked), never closed.
- Once loaded, libtorch can't be unloaded; a libtorch crash still takes down the app (no process isolation).
- Maybe later: one plugin build for both variants (only the shipped libtorch differs). Untested.

